Ugathal, in their inert form, are pink, fleshy creatures made of ropes of muscle and sinew twisted into a vaguely humanoid shape. They can steal the bodies of their victims, extracting the flesh off a corpse and wearing it as disguise.

An Ugathal inside a stolen body passes for a person until it chooses to stop. Then it swells by a foot and a half, its clothing sinks into its flesh, its eyes withdraw into their sockets, and the rest of the face smooths over into a blank, mouthless mask. They laugh with a wet gargling sound.

Cut one open and there is no blood inside, only more flesh. They give no sign of feeling a wound even as their bodies come apart; a blade opens them, while a hammer blow hardly marks them. What they catch, they hold, and a held victim is pinned in place while a proboscis grows from the blank face to drink their blood.

Ugathals are intelligent, but their intelligence serves hunting rather than building anything. They are solitary and do not work in groups, and they keep to deep forests rather than entering cities. Their like appears in rural folklore as the body-snatcher, the warning not to walk alone in the woods at night, because the thing that comes back wearing your friend's face is an Ugathal. Some arcanists believe a mad wizard made the first of them in the Dark Ages, before Aurelia widened the crack, as a weapon of war or espionage; whatever their origin, they are now mostly feral.

An Ugathal cannot copy a face at will. It can only wear the face of someone it has killed, so any man's face an Ugathal is seen wearing belongs to a person it murdered. Two of them wore the shapes of [[Org/Blackline/index|Blackline]] officers in [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/index|Hallia]], with Blackline badges lodged inside their flesh, which means someone tasked them and pointed them at the city, and it is not known yet whether that someone is Blackline or another party using its name.
